FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2704 — `interpolation_inner_start` treats *any* balanced `{…}` inside a string literal as an interpolation, but the parser only does so when the contents re-parse as one complete expression (`src/parser.rs:2936-2941`, `src/parser.rs:3068-3072`). So completion now offers scope names/builtins inside plain text that the parser deliberately keeps as text, e.g. the SPEC's own `json.parse("{ \"name\": \"Alice\" }")` (`SPEC.md:1610`): the braces are balanced, the caret inside them is "inside text", yet `completions` returns `interpolation_completions`. This contradicts the documented contract at `src/lsp.rs:2486` ("Inside a string literal no code can be written, so the answer is empty") and the existing `edge_completions_inside_string_literal_are_empty` intent. — Fix: gate on the parser's actual validity rule (reuse `interpolated_parts`/`read_interpolated`, or require the inner slice to lex/parse as one expression) instead of brace balance alone. — VERIFIED: traced `completions` → `text_start_at` → `interpolation_inner_start` (brace counter only) against `Parser::interpolated_parts`, which pushes a literal `{` and answers `Expr::Text` when `read_interpolated` returns `None`; the JSON example's inner text ` "name": "Alice" ` leaves `inner.current() != Eof`, so it is text to the parser but interpolation to the LSP.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2755 — `EXPRESSION_KEYWORDS` omits `this`, so inside a method the SPEC example `say "I'm {this.name}"` (`SPEC.md:694`) cannot be completed: `this` is a keyword, not a lexical binding, builtin, module, or constant, so it is absent from `analysis.visible` and from the hard-coded list. The feature claims completion inside interpolation but offers an expression that is legal per the spec incompletely. — Fix: include `this` (and verify the list against `is_expression_start`) in the expression keyword set.

3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:2493 — the interpolation branch returns `interpolation_completions` before `member_slot` is consulted, so a member slot written inside an interpolation (`"{person.}"`, `"{this.}"`, `"{files.}"`) falls back to a flat lexical list instead of the object/module members the same slot offers outside text. This makes the new capability unable to complete the most common interpolated expressions from the spec (`{this.name}`, `{problem.message}`). — Fix: when the caret is in an interpolation, still run member-slot detection over the interpolation's decoded inner text and answer `member_completions` there.

4. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:2710 — the raw-character scan skips only two chars for every backslash, so a `\u007B` / `\u007D` escape (decoded to `{`/`}` by `Lexer::read_text`, `src/lexer.rs:331` + `read_unicode_escape`) is invisible to `interpolation_inner_start`: `say "\u007Bname}"` is an interpolation to the parser but plain text to completion, and the reverse can mis-pair braces. — Fix: scan the decoded literal (with an offset map) or special-case the `\uXXXX` escape so the LSP's brace view matches the parser's decoded view.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
